/-
# Unipotent groups of bounded index in characteristic zero (Kourovka Notebook Problem 2.63)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 2.63 (B. I. Plotkin): let `G` be a group of
automorphisms of a vector space over a field of characteristic zero, and suppose that all elements
of `G` are unipotent, with uniformly bounded unipotency indices. Must such a group be locally
finitely stable?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.CharZero.Defs
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.StableSeriesShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BoundedUnipotentGroupStableKourovka2Problem63Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 2.63 (B. I.
Plotkin). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700133 (`KOU-2.63`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A set of linear automorphisms is stable when some finite chain of subspaces `0 = W₀ ≤ W₁ ≤ ⋯ ≤ W_m =
V` satisfies `g w - w ∈ W_i` for every element `g` of the set and every `w ∈ W_{i+1}` (a chain of
additive subgroups with this property spans such a chain of subspaces, so nothing depends on using
subspaces). Groups of automorphisms of `V` are subgroups of the group `V ≃ₗ[K] V` of linear
automorphisms. `HasStableSeries` is shared with the other stable-series entries in
`WantedExt.GroupTheory.StableSeriesShared`. Uniformly bounded unipotency means `(g - 1)ᵐ = 0` for
every `g ∈ G` with one `m`. Locally finitely stable: the subgroup generated by any finite subset of
`G` has a finite stable series. A second target states the Archive's stronger assertion that `G`
itself is finitely stable.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.StableSeriesShared

/-- Kourovka 2.63 (B. I. Plotkin): a group `G` of automorphisms of a vector space over a field of
characteristic `0` all of whose elements are unipotent with uniformly bounded index
(`(g - 1)ᵐ = 0` for all `g ∈ G`, `m` fixed) is locally finitely stable: every finitely generated
subgroup of `G` has a finite stable series. -/
def boundedUnipotentGroupLocallyFinitelyStable : Prop :=
  ∀ (K : Type) [Field K] [CharZero K] (V : Type) [AddCommGroup V] [Module K V]
    (G : Subgroup (V ≃ₗ[K] V)) (m : ℕ), (∀ g ∈ G, ((g : V →ₗ[K] V) - 1) ^ m = 0) →
      ∀ s : Finset (V ≃ₗ[K] V), (s : Set (V ≃ₗ[K] V)) ⊆ G →
        HasStableSeries (Subgroup.closure (s : Set (V ≃ₗ[K] V)) : Set (V ≃ₗ[K] V))

/-- The Archive's strengthening of Kourovka 2.63: such a group `G` is finitely stable, that is,
`G` itself has a finite stable series. -/
def boundedUnipotentGroupFinitelyStable : Prop :=
  ∀ (K : Type) [Field K] [CharZero K] (V : Type) [AddCommGroup V] [Module K V]
    (G : Subgroup (V ≃ₗ[K] V)) (m : ℕ), (∀ g ∈ G, ((g : V →ₗ[K] V) - 1) ^ m = 0) →
      HasStableSeries (G : Set (V ≃ₗ[K] V))

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 2.63 "Yes, it must", which follows from Heineken (1962).
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 2.63 (B. I.
Plotkin); resolution: H. Heineken, Arch. Math. (Basel) 13 (1962), 29-37; E. I. Zel'manov, Math.
USSR-Sb. 66 (1990), no. 1, 159-168, Kourovka Archive, Problem 2.63: Yes, it must, which follows from
Heineken; moreover, such a group is even finitely stable, as follows from Zel'manov,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted boundedUnipotentGroupLocallyFinitelyStable_holds :
    boundedUnipotentGroupLocallyFinitelyStable

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive adds that such a group is even finitely stable, as follows from
Zel'manov (1990). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory:
The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem
2.63 (B. I. Plotkin); resolution: H. Heineken, Arch. Math. (Basel) 13 (1962), 29-37; E. I.
Zel'manov, Math. USSR-Sb. 66 (1990), no. 1, 159-168, Kourovka Archive, Problem 2.63: Yes, it must,
which follows from Heineken; moreover, such a group is even finitely stable, as follows from
Zel'manov, https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted boundedUnipotentGroupFinitelyStable_holds :
    boundedUnipotentGroupFinitelyStable

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BoundedUnipotentGroupStableKourovka2Problem63Wanted
